N={
'xmasdinner_dyk':("What If Christmas Dinner Was KFC? 🍗","What if Christmas dinner was totally different where you live? Let's travel the world. America: ham or a big roast turkey. Australia: it's summer there, so seafood and a barbecue on the beach! The UK: roast turkey and Christmas pudding. Mexico: tamales on Christmas Eve. The Philippines: lechon, a whole roast pig. But here's the twist. Japan: millions of families eat KFC fried chicken for Christmas, and they book it weeks ahead! What's on your Christmas table? Comment below, and subscribe for more!"),
'thanksgiving_dyk':("What If Thanksgiving Was in October? 🦃","What if Thanksgiving happened on a totally different day? Let's check. America: the fourth Thursday of November, with turkey and football. Canada: the second Monday of October! Germany: a harvest festival called Erntedankfest, in early October. South Korea: Chuseok, a three day harvest holiday. Japan: Labor Thanksgiving Day, every November twenty third. But here's the twist. Australia: on tiny Norfolk Island, people celebrate an American style Thanksgiving, thanks to American whalers! When do you give thanks? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'presents_dyk':("When Do Kids Open Christmas Presents? 🎁","When do kids open their Christmas presents? It depends where you live! America: December twenty fifth, on Christmas morning. Australia: December twenty fifth too, and then it's off to the beach! Germany: Christmas Eve, December twenty fourth. The Netherlands: December fifth, when Sinterklaas visits. Russia: New Year's Day, from Grandfather Frost. But here's the twist. Spain: many kids wait until January sixth, when the Three Kings bring the gifts! When do you open yours? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'santa_dyk':("What If Santa Was a Witch? 🧹","Who brings the Christmas presents where you live? America: Santa Claus, with his sleigh and reindeer. Australia: Santa in shorts, and in a famous song, his sleigh is pulled by six white kangaroos! The UK: Father Christmas. France: Père Noël. Russia: Ded Moroz, Grandfather Frost, with the Snow Maiden. But here's the twist. Italy: kids also wait for La Befana, a kind old witch who flies on a broomstick in January! Who visits your house? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'blackfriday_dyk':("Is Black Friday the Biggest Sale on Earth? 🛍️","Is Black Friday the biggest shopping day on Earth? Let's see. America: Black Friday, the day after Thanksgiving. Australia: Black Friday too, now one of its biggest sales of the year. The UK: Black Friday as well, borrowed from the US. Brazil: Black Friday often stretches into a whole Black November! Mexico: El Buen Fin, its own big sales weekend in November. But here's the twist. China: Singles' Day, on November eleventh, has been bigger than Black Friday and Cyber Monday combined! Do you shop on Black Friday? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'newyear_dyk':("What If You Ate 12 Grapes at Midnight? 🍇","What if New Year's Eve meant smashing plates? Let's look around the world. America: the famous ball drop in Times Square. Australia: huge fireworks over Sydney Harbour. Spain: eat twelve grapes, one for every bell at midnight! Japan: temple bells ring one hundred and eight times. Brazil: wear white and jump over seven waves for luck. But here's the twist. Denmark: people smash old plates on their friends' doors for good luck! How do you celebrate? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'emergency_dyk':("What If 911 Didn't Work? 🚨","What if nine one one didn't work where you live? Here's who to call. America: nine one one. Australia: triple zero. The UK: nine nine nine. New Zealand: one one one. Japan: one one nine, for fire and ambulance. But here's the twist. Germany: one one two works all across the European Union, and many mobile phones will connect it in other countries too! What's your emergency number? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'penny_dyk':("What If Pennies Disappeared? 🪙","What if pennies just disappeared? It's already happening! New Zealand: one and two cent coins, gone in nineteen ninety. Australia: gone in nineteen ninety two. Finland: no one or two cent euro coins, so prices are rounded instead. The Netherlands: rounding too, since two thousand four. Canada: said goodbye to the penny in twenty thirteen. But here's the twist. America: the US Mint made its very last penny in twenty twenty five! Do you still use pennies? Comment below, and subscribe!"),

'xmasholiday_dyk':("What If Christmas Wasn't a Day Off? 📅","What if Christmas was just a normal work day? Let's check. America: Christmas Day is a federal holiday. Australia: two days off, Christmas and Boxing Day! The UK: two days off too, Christmas and Boxing Day. South Korea: Christmas is a public holiday. China: Christmas is a normal work day. But here's the twist. Japan: Christmas isn't a day off either, even though cities are covered in lights! Do you get Christmas off? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'voltage_dyk':("What If Your Charger Didn't Fit? 🔌","What if your charger didn't fit the wall? Let's travel with a plug. America: one hundred twenty volts, with a flat two pin plug. Canada: one hundred twenty volts too, with the same plug. The UK: two hundred thirty volts, and a chunky three pin plug with a fuse inside! Australia: two hundred thirty volts, with slanted pins. Switzerland: two hundred thirty volts, and its own unique plug. But here's the twist. Japan: just one hundred volts, and the country runs on two different electricity frequencies! What plug do you use? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'vat_dyk':("What If Sales Tax Was 25%? 🧾","What if every price had a twenty five percent tax? Let's compare. America: no national sales tax, just state and local taxes. Canada: a five percent federal GST, plus provincial taxes. Japan: a ten percent consumption tax. Australia: a ten percent GST, already included in the price. The UK: twenty percent VAT. But here's the twist. Denmark: twenty five percent VAT on almost everything, even food! How much tax do you pay? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'dst_dyk':("What If the Clocks Never Changed? ⏰","What if the clocks never changed? Let's check. America: clocks change twice a year, except in Hawaii and most of Arizona. Australia: only some states change their clocks. The UK: yes, it switches to British Summer Time. Japan: no, the clocks never change. China: no, it stopped in nineteen ninety one. But here's the twist. Russia: tried permanent summer time in twenty eleven, then gave it up in twenty fourteen! Should clocks still change? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'timezones_dyk':("Which Country Has the Most Time Zones? 🕐","Which country has the most time zones? Let's count. China: just one time zone for the whole country! India: one time zone too, and it's half an hour off from most of the world. Australia: three main time zones. America: six time zones across its fifty states. Russia: eleven time zones! Think that's the most? Here's the twist. France: twelve time zones, thanks to its islands and territories all over the world! How many does your country have? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'dateformat_dyk':("Is It 12/11 or 11/12? 📅","Is December eleventh written twelve eleven, or eleven twelve? It depends where you live! America: month first, then the day. Australia: day first, then the month. The UK: day first too. Japan: year first, then month, then day. China: year first as well. But here's the twist. Canada: you can find all three formats, sometimes on the same street! How do you write the date? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'metric_dyk':("What If America Used Kilometers? 📏","What if everyone used kilometers? Let's compare. America: miles, feet, pounds, and Fahrenheit. Canada: kilometers on the road, but people still say their height in feet! Australia: fully metric since the nineteen seventies. Japan: metric, but rooms are often measured in tatami mats. India: switched to metric in the late nineteen fifties. But here's the twist. The UK: metric in shops, but road signs are still in miles, and beer comes in pints! Miles or kilometers? Comment below, and subscribe!"),

'islands_dyk':("Which Country Has the Most Islands? 🏝️","Which country has the most islands? Let's count up. The Philippines: about seven thousand six hundred islands. Australia: over eight thousand two hundred. Japan: about fourteen thousand. Indonesia: more than seventeen thousand! America: around eighteen thousand six hundred. Think that's a lot? Here's the twist. Sweden: about two hundred sixty seven thousand islands, the most in the world! How many islands does your country have? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'hottest_dyk':("Where Is the Hottest Place on Earth? 🔥","Where is the hottest place on Earth? Let's turn up the heat. The UK: forty point three degrees Celsius, in twenty twenty two. Spain: forty seven point six. Canada: forty nine point six! Australia: fifty point seven. Kuwait: fifty three point nine! Think that's hot? Here's the twist. America: Death Valley hit fifty six point seven degrees, that's one hundred thirty four Fahrenheit, the hottest ever recorded! What's the hottest it gets where you live? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'coldest_dyk':("Where Is the Coldest Town on Earth? 🥶","How cold can it get where people live? Let's find out. Australia: minus twenty three degrees Celsius. The UK: minus twenty seven point two. Norway: minus fifty one point four! America: minus sixty two point two, in Alaska. Canada: minus sixty three, in the Yukon! Think that's cold? Here's the twist. Russia: minus sixty seven point eight degrees, and people still live there all year! How cold does it get where you live? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'biggest_dyk':("Is America the Biggest Country? 🗺️","Is America the biggest country in the world? Let's measure. Australia: about seven point seven million square kilometers. Brazil: eight and a half million. China: nine point six million. America: nine point eight million. Canada: almost ten million! Think that's big? Here's the twist. Russia: seventeen point one million square kilometers, almost twice the size of Canada! How big is your country? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'population_dyk':("Which Country Has the Most People? 👥","Which country has the most people? Let's count. Australia: about twenty seven million. Japan: around one hundred twenty four million. Indonesia: about two hundred eighty million. America: around three hundred forty million. China: about one point four one billion! Think China is number one? Here's the twist. India: about one point four five billion people, the biggest population on Earth since twenty twenty three! How many people live in your country? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'sheep_dyk':("More Sheep Than People? 🐑","What if there were more animals than people where you live? America: about eighty seven million cows, but way more people. India: over one hundred ninety million cattle, but about one point four billion people. The UK: around thirty one million sheep. Ireland: about seven million cattle, more cows than people! New Zealand: more than four sheep for every person! Think that's wild? Here's the twist. Australia: there are more kangaroos than people! What animal wins where you live? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'nationalanimal_dyk':("What If Your National Animal Was a Unicorn? 🦄","What if your national animal was a unicorn? Let's check. America: the bald eagle. Australia: the kangaroo, right on the coat of arms. Canada: the beaver! New Zealand: the kiwi, a bird that can't fly. India: the Bengal tiger. But here's the twist. The UK: Scotland's national animal is the unicorn! What's your national animal? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'tallestbuilding_dyk':("Where Is the Tallest Building? 🏙️","Where is the tallest building on Earth? Let's go up. The UK: the Shard, three hundred ten meters. Australia: Q one on the Gold Coast, three hundred twenty two meters. America: One World Trade Center, five hundred forty one meters. China: Shanghai Tower, six hundred thirty two meters. Malaysia: Merdeka one eighteen, six hundred seventy nine meters! Think that's tall? Here's the twist. Dubai: the Burj Khalifa, eight hundred twenty eight meters! What's the tallest building near you? Comment below, and subscribe!"),

'tuition_dyk':("What If College Was Free? 🎓","What if college was free? Let's compare. America: about eleven thousand six hundred dollars a year at a public college, before aid. The UK: about nine thousand five hundred pounds a year in England. Australia: students can use a government loan, and pay it back once they earn enough. Japan: about five hundred thirty six thousand yen a year at national universities. France: only about one hundred seventy five euros a year! But here's the twist. Germany: public universities are free, even for most international students! How much is college where you live? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'schoolage_dyk':("What If School Started at 7? 🎒","What if school didn't start until you were seven? Let's compare. The UK: kids can start school at four in England! Australia: around five. America: kindergarten at five or six. Japan: elementary school at six. Germany: school at six too. But here's the twist. Finland: kids start school at seven, and Finland is still famous for great education! When did you start school? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'summerbreak_dyk':("What If Summer Break Was 3 Months? ☀️","What if summer break lasted three months? Let's compare. Australia: about six weeks, over Christmas, because it's summer there! The UK: about six weeks. Japan: about six weeks, from late July. Germany: six weeks. America: around ten to eleven weeks! Think that's long? Here's the twist. Italy: about thirteen weeks, from mid June to mid September! How long is your summer break? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'cash_dyk':("What If Cash Disappeared? 💵","What if cash disappeared? Let's see who still uses it. Germany: still loves cash! Japan: still uses lots of cash too. America: most people pay by card now. Australia: cash is only about thirteen percent of payments. The UK: around twelve percent. But here's the twist. Sweden: fewer than one in ten store purchases use cash, and some shops won't take it at all! Cash or card? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'speedfine_dyk':("What If Speeding Tickets Matched Your Salary? 🚔","What if your speeding ticket depended on how much you earn? America: usually a flat fine, the same for everyone. Australia: a flat fine too. The UK: courts can set fines based on your weekly income. Germany: day fines, based on income, for serious offenses. Switzerland: income based, with fines over one hundred thousand dollars! But here's the twist. Finland: one millionaire got a speeding ticket of one hundred twenty one thousand euros! Should fines depend on income? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'gamble_dyk':("What If Casinos Banned Locals? 🎰","What if you weren't allowed into a casino in your own country? America: you usually need to be twenty one. Australia: eighteen. The UK: eighteen too. Japan: no casinos yet, the first one is planned in Osaka. Singapore: locals pay a one hundred fifty dollar entry fee! But here's the twist. South Korea: locals are banned from every casino except one! Would you go to a casino? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'trains_dyk':("What If America Had Bullet Trains? 🚄","What if America had bullet trains everywhere? Let's compare. Australia: no high speed rail at all. America: the new Acela reaches one hundred sixty miles per hour. Germany: ICE trains at three hundred kilometers per hour. France: TGV trains at three hundred twenty. Japan: the Shinkansen at three hundred twenty too. But here's the twist. China: over forty five thousand kilometers of high speed rail, more than the rest of the world combined! Would you ride a bullet train? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
'fries_dyk':("Are French Fries Actually French? 🍟","Are French fries actually French? Let's ask around. America: French fries. The UK: chips, and potato chips are called crisps! Australia: hot chips. Canada: poutine, fries with gravy and cheese curds. The Netherlands: patat, often with mayo! But here's the twist. Belgium: Belgians say fries were invented there, not in France! What do you call them? Comment below, and subscribe!"),
}
